I've been asked before whether I would  consider a physics PhD to be worth it  and that's kind of a hard question to  answer because it's such a personal one  it really depends what you hope to get  out of doing a PhD I certainly wouldn't  recommend just jumping into one straight  after undergrad because you see it as  the next step or the next thing to do  I'd really consider why you're doing it  it can be a big cost to you in general  if you were to go and get a job straight  out of undergrad you would earn a lot  more money than doing your PhD for the  same amount of time so it really has to  be worth it in terms of do you think you  will make an excellent researcher do you  want to pursue a more academic line and  if you do then maybe consider it further  but here's some thoughts for my friends  who are all doing a PhD in physics at  the moment it might be a little bit  biased but here's what they had to say  there's a PhD worth it well as a PhD  student myself I would say yes mainly  because I think I like the challenge and  I like the problem-solving and the  skills that you learn from it are really  useful basically if you're interested in  figuring out more about the universe and  figure out how stuff works the phd's  really useful there's valuable things  that I've gained definitely getting a  new attitude to teamwork I mean you know  group assignments were always the worst  in undergraduate studies but being able  to work in a big group on so sometimes  it seems like your individual project is  not going to change the world right but  when you're working when your individual  project is part of a larger project and  that larger project has a very clear  goal of we're going to we're gonna work  really hard to try and figure out what  is going on with the inter nuclear  potential or something like some big  project which one person alone can't do  but you put a bunch of people together  and  and you can all work towards some common  goal that's something that has  definitely been worth it and then yeah  personally I've learned a lot of new  things I've gained a bit more confidence  in that I can't keep learning more thick  new things and I know how to learn so  for me so far it's definitely worth it I  think that's a matter of perspective  I've certainly drawn a lot from the  experience and a lot of people that I've  spoken to seem to be enjoying it in fact  on a recent survey from nature despite  all of the difficulties of doing  research something like 75 percent of  people are satisfied with the experience  so obviously there's something valuable  there and it is something that comes at  a cost but if that it cost buys you the  satisfaction of understanding something  complicated and answering questions that  nobody has asked before then you know  that's that's enough for me and that may  be if that's enough for you  of course it's absolutely worth it like  the amount of transferable skills that  you can get from a physics degree is  really mind-boggling especially nowadays  given it's a big data sort of economy  the amount of data that physicists  especially I guess like myself I'm a  nuclear physicist we have to deal with a  large amount of data that's a huge skill  to gain also plainly being able to  understand mathematical models being  able to write down a set of differential  equations that might model some scenario  allows you to go into economics to some  small degree if you want to as well so I  highly recommend a physics degree even  as a generalist degree like it's a  really good generalist degree I think  [Music]  you
 

